NNAAMMEE
     aarrpp — address resolution display and control

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aarrpp [----lliibbxxoo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s] [--nn] [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e] _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
     aarrpp [----lliibbxxoo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s] [--nn] [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e] --aa
     aarrpp --dd _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e [ppuubb]
     aarrpp --dd [--ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e] --aa
     aarrpp --ss _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp] [bbllaacckkhhoollee | rreejjeecctt] [ppuubb]
     aarrpp --SS _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp] [bbllaacckkhhoollee | rreejjeecctt] [ppuubb]
     aarrpp --ff _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aarrpp utility displays and modifies the Internet-to-Ethernet address
     translation tables used by the address resolution protocol (arp(4)).
     With no flags, the program displays the current ARP entry for _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e.
     The host may be specified by name or by number, using Internet dot nota‐
     tion.

     Available options:

     ----lliibbxxoo
             Generate output via libxo(3) in a selection of different human
             and machine readable formats.  See xo_parse_args(3) for details
             on command line arguments.

     --aa      The program displays or, if it is used with the --dd flag, deletes
             all of the current ARP entries.

     --dd      A super-user may delete an entry for the host called _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
             with the --dd flag.  If the ppuubb keyword is specified, only the
             “published” ARP entry for this host will be deleted.

             Alternatively, the --dd flag may be combined with the --aa flag to
             delete all entries.

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             Limit the operation scope to the ARP entries on _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e.  Ap‐
             plicable only to the following operations: display one, display
             all, delete all.

     --nn      Show network addresses as numbers (normally aarrpp attempts to dis‐
             play addresses symbolically).

     --ss _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r
             Create an ARP entry for the host called _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e with the Ether‐
             net address _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r.  The Ethernet address is given as six hex
             bytes separated by colons.  The entry will be permanent unless
             the word tteemmpp is given in the command.  If the word ppuubb is given,
             the entry will be “published”; i.e., this system will act as an
             ARP server, responding to requests for _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e even though the
             host address is not its own.  In this case the _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r can be
             given as aauuttoo in which case the interfaces on this host will be
             examined, and if one of them is found to occupy the same subnet,
             its Ethernet address will be used.

             If the rreejjeecctt keyword is specified the entry will be marked so
             that traffic to the host will be discarded and the sender will be
             notified the host is unreachable.  The bbllaacckkhhoollee keyword is simi‐
             lar in that traffic is discarded but the sender is not notified.
             These can be used to block external traffic to a host without us‐
             ing a firewall.

     --SS _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r
             Is just like --ss except any existing ARP entry for this host will
             be deleted first.

     --ff _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Cause the file _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e to be read and multiple entries to be set
             in the ARP tables.  Entries in the file should be of the form

                   _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e _e_t_h_e_r___a_d_d_r [tteemmpp] [bbllaacckkhhoollee | rreejjeecctt] [ppuubb]

             with argument meanings as given above.  Leading whitespace and
             empty lines are ignored.  A ‘#’ character will mark the rest of
             the line as a comment.

